WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.618
Scale the bottleneck you actually have.

00:00:02.618 --> 00:00:08.795
Application replication, database read replicas,
larger machines and caching solve different

00:00:08.795 --> 00:00:09.399
problems.

00:00:09.399 --> 00:00:14.703
Decide which reads tolerate staleness and which
responses can be shared safely.

00:00:14.703 --> 00:00:21.350
A stateless monolith can run across many instances;
deployment scaling does not by itself require a

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.633
change to microservice boundaries.

00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:29.031
Convert workload claims into rates, then inspect the
assumptions behind those rates.

00:00:29.031 --> 00:00:34.301
Monthly averages hide peaks and user actions are not
identical to disk operations.

00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:39.057
The case scales favorably because many responses can
be shared and cached.

00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:45.291
Validate those properties, model the limiting
resources and measure representative traffic before

00:00:45.291 --> 00:00:47.733
promising a particular scaling factor.
